                <pb facs="0453" n="444" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg444"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 Biddle, Anthony J. D./242: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Deputy Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Biddle</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bordeaux</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-06-27T17:00:00+02:00">June 27, 1940—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 28—7 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>105. Your telegram No. 34, June 24, 11 a.m. The Government now plans to leave
                    Saturday morning early for Royat near Clermont-Ferrand. The Foreign Office will
                    be located at La Bourboule some 35 miles away and the Diplomatic Corps at
                    Mont-Dore some 5 miles from the latter.</p>
                <p>No instructions have yet been received with respect to my staff.</p>
                <p>Unless contrary instructions, therefore, are received by Friday evening they will
                    all, including the Military and Naval Attachés, proceed Saturday morning to
                    Mont-Dore.</p>
                <p>Bordeaux will probably be formally “occupied” by the Germans Saturday or
                    Sunday.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Biddle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
